\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Simplified event log with two truck visits. Case attributes such as the number of planned stops are stored once per case and are not shown.}}{9}{table.caption.10}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Components of a ProSiT simulation model (version 1.0.4). Times are counted in open minutes of the relevant calendar.}}{19}{table.caption.12}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Research directions and the questions they leave open for the CTB case study.}}{24}{table.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Operational data sources used for event-log engineering.}}{27}{table.caption.15}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Visit structure and turnaround time (TTT) by partition. Approach is the time from Gate In to the start of the first stop, exit the time from the end of the last stop to Gate Out, and transfer the time between stops. Because gate events are instantaneous, the four components add up to the TTT.}}{31}{table.caption.16}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Domain rules for truck visits. Source: domain knowledge of the author; confirmation by the terminal pending. The type decides where a rule can be enforced (Section~\ref {sec:exp_rules}).}}{34}{table.caption.17}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Learned components of the final simulation model (ProSiT 1.0.4 on the repaired IM 0.0 state machine, training log). Waiting and execution time are the retrained components of the final configuration (Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_mechanisms}); the inventory of every model is in Appendix~\ref {app:prosit_inventory}.}}{39}{table.caption.20}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Discovery settings of the simulation model.}}{39}{table.caption.21}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Capacities and calendars. The discovered resource calendars cover 122 to 128 of the 168 hours of a week; they are set to the full week so that trucks already inside are served to completion. The gate calendar still controls admission.}}{42}{table.caption.27}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces One simulated truck of the final model (configuration of E4, seed 42), step by step. The leaf of each tree follows from the features at that moment; the waiting and execution times are drawn from the distribution of that leaf; the waiting time includes any delay until a lane is free (capacity checked over the whole stop at its start).}}{43}{table.caption.28}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Differences between the ProSiT simulation loop and the final model.}}{44}{table.caption.29}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Experiments, varied factors and the research questions they address.}}{47}{table.caption.31}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Visits per partition for the restricted and the expanded population. Partitions are chronological by Gate In. The validation period starts on 10 April 2026, 15:50, and the test period on 20 April 2026, 18:17. The 184 visits with unknown activity labels remain excluded.}}{52}{table.caption.32}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Discovery configurations of E1, all run in ProM Lite 1.4 on the training partition of the expanded population (58,262 visits). The iDHM frequency threshold is relative to the number of visits: at 0.1, only the 7 of 60 directly-follows relations that occur at least 5,827 times are used. Because no data attributes were selected, iDHM discovers no conditional dependencies, and the conditional heuristic (C4.5 with F1 score, condition threshold 0.5) has no effect.}}{53}{table.caption.33}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces What a noise threshold removes from the training log (59,139 visits, 82 sequences). A sequence below the threshold is noise. Mean TTT of all visits: 37 minutes. P90: 64 minutes. Multi-block visits: visits to two or more RMG blocks.}}{54}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Interventions of E2. $n$: planned stops from the manifest; $c$: stops done. Type after the two kinds of process enhancement (Section~\ref {sec:bg_rules}). Rules 1 and 2 hold in every net. The RMG part of rule 5 is checked in the generated log; rule 7 needs data that are not recorded.}}{56}{table.caption.35}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces $2\times 2$ contrast for IM 0.0 in E3. The same-language state machine without repair is a fifth cell (control). All cells use the same ProSiT settings and seeds.}}{59}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Fixed simulation conditions in E3.}}{61}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Decision matrix of E3.}}{62}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Measures of operational validity in E4. For each load profile in P4, the \emph {profile gap} is the mean absolute difference of the simulated medians to the observed ones over the bins.}}{63}{table.caption.41}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Mechanisms examined in E4. Observed values from the test log; the training log gives the same profiles within one to two minutes.}}{65}{table.caption.42}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces Configurations of E4. ProSiT: component as fitted in E3. No self-count: retrained without counting the stop in its own queue. Lane free: waiting measured from the moment a lane became free. Whole delay: waiting measured from enabling to start. Every configuration from b3 on, and the baseline a0, is also run with an exact count of the activities in progress (suffix x, see the design decisions).}}{68}{table.caption.43}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Nets taken to the rule audit and repair (E2), with their values on the training log. Selection on training values only: sound, at least 90\,\% fitting visits and not dominated in fitting visits and precision. HM 0.8 is also non-dominated but lies between HM 0.7 and HM 0.9 and is left out. ILP 1.00 and IMf 0.3 are added as references for a permissive and a filtered net.}}{74}{table.caption.47}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces What the rare sequences and the sequences a net rejects stand for (training log, 59,139 visits, 82 sequences). Truck-minutes: sum of the turnaround times. Visits $>$ P90: visits with a turnaround time above the training P90 of 64 minutes. Multi-block: visits to two or more RMG blocks (1.75\,\% of the visits). Mean TTT in minutes (all visits: 37). Again in test: sequences that occur again in the test log.}}{74}{table.caption.48}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces ProM replay of the validation partition (14,625 visits) on the five Inductive Miner nets. PM4Py returns the same number of fitting visits and the same total deviation cost (log plus model moves) for every net. Mean trace fitness as reported by ProM. The last column adds the PM4Py precision: the filtered nets reject 5.0 to 7.5\,\% of the visits for at most 0.045 more precision, and IMf 0.4 rejects more than a third.}}{76}{table.caption.49}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Discovered nets on the training partition (58,262 visits) and the validation partition (14,625 visits). Fitting visits: share of visits whose alignment has no deviation. Fitness: alignment-based log fitness. IM: Inductive Miner, IMf with the noise threshold given; HM: iDHM with the dependency threshold given; ILP: ILP Miner with the filter threshold given (0.00 and 0.25 yield the same net). $^{a}$Unsound, values reported as a diagnostic only. Alpha++ has no reachable final marking, so no alignment exists. $^{b}$Reference net that accepts exactly the training sequences (Section~\ref {sec:exp_candidates}).}}{77}{table.caption.50}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Behaviour preserved by the sound nets and the prefix-tree reference. A sequence is accepted if the net replays it without deviation. Multi-block visits handle containers at more than one RMG block (1,008 training and 311 validation visits).}}{80}{table.caption.52}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Rule audit and exact repair for rules 3 and 5 (E2). Rules violated: rules whose violation the language of the net permits, before $\rightarrow $ after the repair (6 (bound): more than four stops; enforced in the simulator by the stop guard, not in the net). Fitting visits (train / test) are the same before and after the repair. Same language: the unrepaired language written as a state machine, the control for the change of encoding. Sequences with 4 stops: distinct sequences the net permits; 5 occur in training.}}{82}{table.caption.54}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Rule audit of the model languages. ``Unbounded'' means that loops allow arbitrarily many yard events.}}{83}{table.caption.55}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Number of distinct sequences permitted by each net, by number of yard events. IM0 additionally permits the empty visit.}}{84}{table.caption.56}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Repaired variants of IM0 on the expanded population. The control encodes the unrestricted IM0 language as a state machine.}}{85}{table.caption.57}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces The same rule restriction (R5, R12) applied to every sound base model. Permitted sequences are counted after the restriction. Validation visits accepted refers to the restricted model.}}{87}{table.caption.59}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.11}{\ignorespaces The four repaired nets taken to E3, ordered by precision. Left: conformance on the training log (E1, E2). Right: simulated visits in the test period with stop guard, means over five seeds. TVD: total-variation distance to the observed test log (0 = identical). RMG visits to 2 blocks: share of the visits with an RMG stop that use two different blocks. The turnaround time is far too long for every net; the cause lies outside the control flow (Section~\ref {sec:res_mechanisms}).}}{88}{table.caption.60}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.12}{\ignorespaces Effect of the rule treatment on the simulated visits (E3), test period, means over five seeds. Original net: IM 0.0 as discovered (parallel branches that silent transitions can skip). Repaired net: language restricted to rules 3 and 5, written as a state machine. Guard: data-aware stop guard for rule 6. Control: the unrepaired language written as a state machine. Overlapping stops: share of the visits with more than one stop. Unseen sequences: generated visits whose sequence never occurs in the training log. TVD: total-variation distance to the distribution of the observed test log (0 = identical).}}{89}{table.caption.61}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.13}{\ignorespaces Generated visits of the three control-flow candidates compared with the validation log. Means over five replications of 14,625 visits. Approach is Gate In to the start of the first stop, exit is the end of the last stop to Gate Out.}}{90}{table.caption.62}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.14}{\ignorespaces ProSiT's waiting-time target at the RMG blocks by the load of the block when the stop is enabled, training log. The target is measured from the moment the block was last completely free (single server).}}{98}{table.caption.63}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.15}{\ignorespaces Error attribution along the path of the truck (E4a--E4d), against the test log, means over five seeds. From b3x on, the observed demand and the observed blocks are used, so that only the queue and the service vary. The rows in italics are differences between real periods. Med., P90: median and P90 of the TTT; CTD: cycle-time distance; Appr., Stops: mean approach and stop time per visit (all in minutes). Top bl.: share of the RMG stops at the busiest block in \%. Max: largest number of trucks at one block at once. Spear.: Spearman correlation of hourly arrivals and hourly median TTT. G-appr., G-stop, G-WIP: profile gaps for the approach by trucks at the block, the RMG stop duration by other trucks at the block and the TTT by trucks in the terminal (minutes).}}{99}{table.caption.64}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.16}{\ignorespaces Choice of the final configuration on the training period (E4f): replayed training visits with their observed blocks, compared with the training log, means over five seeds. Admissible: no rule violation and at most three trucks at a block at once. The configuration with the smallest sum of the profile gaps is chosen ($\leftarrow $). Gaps and CTD in minutes.}}{101}{table.caption.65}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.17}{\ignorespaces Final configuration with each change reversed alone, with other block allocations, other arrival intensities and without replay (E4e), against the test log, means over five seeds. $^{*}$Only the 2,393 visits of the first day, because the queue of the learned block choice grows without bound. Med., P90: median and P90 of the TTT; CTD: cycle-time distance; Appr., Stops: mean approach and stop time per visit (all in minutes). Top bl.: share of the RMG stops at the busiest block in \%. Max: largest number of trucks at one block at once. Spear.: Spearman correlation of hourly arrivals and hourly median TTT. G-appr., G-stop, G-WIP: profile gaps for the approach by trucks at the block, the RMG stop duration by other trucks at the block and the TTT by trucks in the terminal (minutes).}}{102}{table.caption.66}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.18}{\ignorespaces Key values of the final simulation model against the observed logs (replayed visits and observed blocks, means over five seeds). Times in minutes; CTD: cycle-time distance; Spearman: hourly arrivals against hourly median TTT; Max at block: largest number of trucks at one block at once (the log records 4 because of short recording overlaps, Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_mechanisms}).}}{104}{table.caption.67}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.19}{\ignorespaces Load-response profiles against the test log: medians in minutes, means over five seeds. --: fewer than 50 cases in the bin. With three lanes, the bins ``4+ trucks at the block''{} and ``3+ other trucks''{} cannot occur; in the log they are short recording overlaps.}}{106}{table.caption.72}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.20}{\ignorespaces Error of the mean turnaround time (simulated minus observed, minutes) and its parts; the four parts add up to the TTT error up to rounding. The stop error is split into a count part (stops per visit by area, i.e.\ routing) and a duration part (mean stop duration by area). Last row: training log minus test log.}}{106}{table.caption.73}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.21}{\ignorespaces Acceptance criteria of Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_runs} for the final configuration. Thresholds from the differences between real periods: Spearman of the weakest test week; profile gaps (approach / stop duration / TTT by trucks in the terminal, minutes) of the largest difference between weeks; CTD within the differences between weeks (3.1 minutes) or between periods (5.5 minutes).}}{107}{table.caption.74}%
